# reading 2 of kant-practical-1.md by claude-opus-5-5
atom	univ_emp	reason admits only empirical principles (universal empiricism)
atom	divis	mathematics proves the infinite divisibility of space, which empiricism cannot admit
atom	contra	mathematics is in contradiction with a reason that admits only empirical principles
atom	clash	the greatest possible evidence of demonstration is in manifest contradiction with the alleged conclusions from experience
atom	doubt	we are driven to ask, like Cheselden's blind patient, "Which deceives me, sight or touch?"
atom	emp_felt	empiricism is based on a necessity felt, rationalism on a necessity seen
atom	sceptic	universal empiricism reveals itself as absolute scepticism
atom	touchstone	Hume left at least one certain touchstone, which can only be found in a priori principles
atom	hume_abs	it is right to attribute absolute scepticism to Hume in an unqualified sense
P1	S1	divis
# "as it inevitably is": an empiricist reason, faced with the divisibility proof, is in contradiction with mathematics
P2	S1	univ_emp ∧ divis → contra
P3	S1	contra → clash ∧ doubt
P4	S2	emp_felt
# unstated: being driven to doubt which sense deceives us amounts to absolute scepticism
A1	-	doubt → sceptic
C	S3	univ_emp → sceptic	from P1 P2 P3 A1
# S4 qualifies the attribution to Hume; it is a side claim, not part of the main argument
P5	S4	touchstone
P6	S4	touchstone → ¬hume_abs
